`str.toLocaleUpperCase()` gives you title case, but it isn't quite right for English: it capitalizes everything except the last word.

```js
const toTitleCase = (str) => {
  return str
    .split(' ')
    .map(word => {
      if (!word) return '';
      return word[0].toUpperCase() + word.slice(1);
    })
    .join(' ');
};

console.log(toTitleCase('hello world')); // "Hello World"
```

`Array.prototype.splice` mutates an array by removing and/or inserting elements at a given index.

```js
const arr = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'];

// remove up to 3 items starting at index 1
arr.splice(1, 3);
// arr is now ['a', 'd']

// insert two items starting at index 2
arr.splice(2, 0, 'x', 'y');
// arr is now ['a', 'd', 'x', 'y']

// remove one item and replace it
arr.splice(1, 1, 'z');
// arr is now ['a', 'z', 'y']
```

The most common pattern is `splice(index, deleteCount)` — it removes `deleteCount` elements starting at `index`. Anything beyond that (`...items`) are inserted where the deletion happened.
